is_anomaly: true
faulty_devices: r4
diagnosis_text:
Affected component: OSPF virtual-link between r4 and r8. After r4 reloaded, its OSPF lost the virtual-link configuration that connects r8 (ABR for Areas 1 and 2) to the backbone (Area 0) through Area 1.

Mechanism: r8 originates Area 2 routes (18.x.x.x loopbacks and 80.0.0.0/8) and must inject them into Area 0 via a virtual-link to r4 across Area 1. With the virtual-link down on r4, r8’s Area 2 summary-LSAs are not flooded into the backbone, so r1 never installs those prefixes. Area 1 remains healthy, so r1 still learns Area 1 routes (e.g., 58.0.0.0/8) and all Area 0 routes.

Localization:
- r1: OSPF table shows many Area 0/1 routes (e.g., 58.0.0.0/8) but lacks 18.x.x.x and 80.0.0.0/8.
- r8: Running config has “area 1 virtual-link 14.1.4.1” (expects r4 as peer) and shows a VLINK0 neighbor to 14.1.4.1.
- r4: Running config has no virtual-link lines; “show ip ospf neighbor” has no VLINK neighbor; OSPF Area 1 shows “Number of full virtual adjacencies going through this area: 0.”

Root cause: r4 lost the OSPF virtual-link to r8 during the reload (configuration not preserved), severing Area 2’s backbone connectivity.

Corrective fix: On r4, re-add the virtual-link under OSPF: “area 1 virtual-link 18.1.4.1”, then write/save the config. Once the virtual-link forms, r8 will again advertise Area 2 summaries into Area 0 and r1 will relearn the 18.x.x.x and 80.0.0.0/8 routes. 
=== END ===